urving sloped drop like this they  usually don't have a steep straight drop  they usually just have curves everywhere  and the drop is no exception to that  there are a few exceptions like George  in the dragon in the efteling or really  I should say yours in the black I'm a  Dutch person myself but which is also a  really good UCI that I've been on myself  but aside from a few cases most of them  have drops that are almost identical to  the one that I've built here an example  for this drop that I used was actually  El Toro  which is not the El Toro in Six Flags  but the one in flown in Germany which  has a very similar drop and it comes  down into a tunnel underneath a footpath  I think which is the reference that I  used for this drop coming into the  tunnel although the change here is that  after the first drop after this curve  here it dips a little bit lower into  that tunnel so I think that's actually a  really cool element to start off the  ride with so you first get into the main  drop and then right after that you drop  down again down into that tunnel so you  probably catch some air time right after  this drop even if it's not that much and  if it's just for a short amount of time  that is just very typical about GCI  coasters you rarely get very long  sustained airtime but it's usually just  little pops of airtime all throughout  the layouts say that that is one very  typical element about GCI coasters  another one is the station fly through  or is it a station fly by I don't know  either one of those words in any case  what usually happens on these coasters  is that somewhere halfway through the  layouts you have a segment where the  track goes back through the station but  not really this station it just goes  through the station building but a bit  above the actual station with a straight  piece of track which is very cool if  you're on the coaster because you fly  back through the station somewhere  during the middle of the layout which is  really nice but if you're standing in  these stations as I've personally been  able to tell on Troy in toverland they  make a lot of noise it is extremely loud  so yeah it is cool even if it is a bit  uncomfortable if you're standing in the  station itself because the coaster is  passing by  at full speed but yeah it's one of these  typical GCI things that other coaster  manufacturers don't really seem to do as  much and yeah I guess those are the most  typical elements whenever somebody  whenever a coaster enthusiasts would  think of typical elements of a GCI  coaster I think that the station fly  throughs and the curve drops are just  about the most typical things I've also  seen some other quirky elements I've  seen that recent GCI coasters tend to  have two planks on the middle of the  track but to be completely honest I have  no idea what these planks are for  exactly maybe it's just to support the  track a bit more keep it more steady but  really I'm not sure but there's a GCI  called gold striker which is I believe  in California's Great America but I'm  not entirely sure it's in the US in any  case it could yeah I think it's that  Park but yeah it has also two planks  running down the middle of the track for  instance I don't know entirely sure why  that is  I've seen it on some other coaster  manufacturers as well but it seems to be  more of a recent trend but yeah that is  just about it and then there's the  support work which is very typical as  well especially around all of these  curves because they tend to fan out from  the curves and have these diagonal beams  in between all of these squares so the  method that I use to make all of these  supports is basically to use the the  align to scenery tool to align these  planks to the track which is a bit  finicky because you have to align them  using the wooden supports but it's hard  to get that placement it's highly right  and then from those slanted beams I use  the horizontal beams to connect these  together then you get some squares and  then I fill in these squares with  diagonal beams like this and finally add  some vertical beams right in the middle  of that to connect back into the ground  and I'm going to skip ahead a little bit  here actually quite a bit too when all  of these supports are done to show the  last step which is basically to add the  footers  which is the small concrete elements at  the bottom of all of the supports to  hold them up and I really have to thank  feed the bear here because he was  willing to take up some of the supports  of gambits even if for a bit because  going back there I pretty much figured  out what the best way to go about  recreating these supports in planet  coaster was and that is just to take the  arch shapes and color them in just about  the same color as the in-game footers so  these are not quite as detailed as the  in-game ones but I do think they work  better than any of the footers that  we've gotten from the support pieces in  the game it doesn't have to be too  complicated I think it doesn't have to  be super detailed in fact I don't know  if I want it to be detailed because well  the thinking of the park for poor old  geek ISM and masked bandits who have  been waiting for me for weeks to try and  play some wooden beams um no the more I  can keep this low-poly the better it  would also be because this is a park in  the end so I do have to consider how  many pieces that I'm placing here so  it's not too lag of the game too much  even though the pavilion that they've  just built that they've got built from  somebody else who is also a lot of  pieces but for my parts I'm gonna try  and keep this as you know low as I can  get it in terms of the item counts even  though at the end I can already spoil  that it's definitely going to contain  quite a few thousand wooden beams which  are all individually placed almost all  individually placed so yeah again if you  ever think about building a GCI and  planet coaster take my word of advice  don't but if you really really want to  do it I guess some of the tips that I've  given in this video you know to try and  look up real POVs of GCI coasters place  you're shaping on that and do the  supports pretty much the way I have been  doing it in this video and then you  should be golden  now I'm gonna end the video here because  I didn't want the video to become too  long I'm gonna continue it in the next  one which is going to pick up where this  one left off and yeah I hope to see you  guys next time  should be coming out soon I just didn't  want to make a single video of over an  hour long thanks for watching and I hope  to see you next time
 

